1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,824
Where does a card number live, and how does the rest of the bank use a card without ever seeing it?

2
00:00:04,874 --> 00:00:09,840
A card's first digits, its BIN, name the scheme and the issuing bank.

3
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:18,288
Both schemes publish their BIN lists, so the BIN module in card-vault syncs them through the adapters into its own schema, at startup and every night.

4
00:00:18,338 --> 00:00:25,416
The sync is idempotent, and a scheme that doesn't answer, or answers with nothing, keeps its old list.

5
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:31,008
A range the schemes' lists don't carry yet can be added by hand, and no sync removes it.

6
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:40,992
Lookups never touch the database: an in-memory index cuts a card's first digits from the longest known BIN length down to the shortest, and the first match wins.

7
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:49,104
A bank directory in the configuration names each issuer in English, Russian and Uzbek, and without it card-vault doesn't start.

8
00:00:49,154 --> 00:00:58,416
Before any scheme is asked, the card module checks the Luhn digit, then asks the BIN module for the scheme, sending only the first eight digits.

9
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:02,448
A plain card number in a database would be a disaster on the day of a breach.

10
00:01:02,498 --> 00:01:10,344
So card numbers live in one place only: card-vault, in the separate cards unit, with its own Vault, Keycloak and Postgres.

11
00:01:10,394 --> 00:01:21,624
Other units hold a card token instead and call card-vault over mutual T L S, where each operation needs its own role: bind, read, debit, reports or admin.

12
00:01:21,674 --> 00:01:30,000
Card data is sealed by Vault Transit: card-vault sends a value in and keeps only the ciphertext, so the keys never leave Vault.

13
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:37,416
Each value is sealed with a context naming its column and its card, so a ciphertext copied to another row doesn't open.

14
00:01:37,466 --> 00:01:45,120
Rotation is Vault's own: new values take the new key version, older ones still open, and a rewrap moves them forward.

15
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:56,664
To find a card without decrypting anything, the vault keeps a blind index, a Transit HMAC of the number, pinned to one key version so the same number always gives the same index.

16
00:01:56,714 --> 00:02:04,056
When Vault is down or refuses, card-vault fails closed with card processor unavailable, and never guesses.

17
00:02:04,106 --> 00:02:09,048
The database itself refuses any card value that isn't a Vault ciphertext.

18
00:02:09,098 --> 00:02:16,176
A number is decrypted only to hand it to the card's scheme, and every use of card data is written to an audit trail.

19
00:02:16,226 --> 00:02:25,296
Bank admins reach cards and the BIN directory only through the API, with the cards admin role, and a search by number answers only the masked number.

20
00:02:25,346 --> 00:02:30,240
Next, the payment itself, starting with a request nobody can forge.
